  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nThe central idea is to use least-angle optimization (L1Boost) tailored to the non-smooth structure of the Erd??s problem. Instead of local optimization, we directly target the local minima of the constraint for pointwise optimality, using an alternating pattern construction that inherently satisfies constraints. The Erd??s discrepancy is minimized by enforcing \"local smoothness\" through projected gradient steps, generalized via an exponential smoothing penalty for lag contributions. While less intuitive than prior strategies, this leverages the high accuracy of exponential moving averages in integer programming formulations and provides robust convergence comparable to Adam but with better computational scaling.\n\nMathematical specification:\nVariables: Piecewise constant h: [0,2] \u2192 [0,1] represented with N grid points (even). Parameters: Regularization parameter \u03b2 (\u22481e3\u20131e4). Transformation: Represent h's as deviations from a baseline symmetric mean, subject to redistributive constraints.\nObjective: Minimize max_{k}  \u222b[h(x)(1-h(x+k))] dx\nApproximation: - Use finite element approximation of integrals via Simpson's rule on grid points.\nConstraint: \u222bh dx = 1, with 0\u2264h(i)\u22641 for all i.\nPenalty formulation: L1Boost style combinatorial structure. We solve:\n  minimize_h ||\u2207_k \u222b[h(x)(1-h(x+k))] dx ||_\u221e\nvia:\n  h_{t+1} = Project_h_constrained(h_t + \u03b2 \u2207F(h_t))\nThe projected gradient requires an explicit formulation of the functional dependency: The correlation structure is exploited by decomposing contributions to F(h) via Positive-Term Interaction (PTI) matrix.\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Input: n_grid (e.g., 572), initial_h_values (seed solution)\n2. Set dx = 2.0 / n_grid\n3. Represent h as permutation (can be randomized) of [1,1,...,0,0] (N/2 ones)\n4. Compute baseline grid values by interpolating initial_h_values (if available)\n5. Initialize:\n   - Projection function: project_to_domain(h) = (h / \u2211h*2) * (N/2) [scaled and constrained]\n   - Penalty matrix G (size N \u00d7 L) containing partial derivatives of F with respect to each h[i]\n   - Optimization loop:\n        t=1 to iterations\n        While ||\u2207F||_\u221e > threshold or improvement\n            \u2207F = compute_gradient(h) [using Soft-L\u221e approximation]\n            h_candidate = h + \u03b2 * \u2207F  [non-normalized update]\n            h_new = project_to_domain(h_candidate)\n            If (F(h_new) < F(h)): accept\n            Else: perturbation step (maintain local optimum)\n6. Gradient computation:\n   We approximate F(h) \u2248 max\u2096 (simulated integral via Simpson) with:\n        \u2207F[i] = \u2211\u2096_{\u2207 contribution noncontact} \u2202F/\u2202h[i] * \u03c9\u2096\n   The derivative \u2202F/\u2202h[i] at (i, k) is computed based on position overlap with phase window.\n\nSearch choices:\n- Grid size: Start with 572 (already given) and scale by factors 2 (to 1144), random perturbation of order 0.1 on the parent grid.\n- \u03b2 = 1e4 (default), dynamically adjusted to learning annealing schedule (cosine decay from 1e4 to 1e0)\n- Max iterations: 2000, with early break if improvement < 1e-4 for 50 steps\n- Projection frequency: every 20 updates and at start/end\n\nBudgeting robustness:\n- Main cost: O(n_grid^2) for correlation matrix + O(n_grid) gradient updates.\n- Estimate: 572 grid \u21d2 ~3e5 operations per full optimization, with Adam tuning only requiring ~10% of total time.\n- Time break: At 95% remaining time, switch to projection-only phase.\n\nValidation return:\n- h: valid domain [0,1]\u00d7\u2211h*dx=1\n- C5: computed using scikit-integrate's \u4e8c\u7ef4Simpson rule over the shift kernel\n- Projected scores: via h_new = (h[i] * core_rank) algorithm\n\nAfter each optimization:\n- Assess stability of solution via cross-constraint: h[i]\u2208[0,1], \u2211h*dx=1\n- De-bias using high learning phase then low",
